# cpmtools disk format definitions for the images in this directory.
#
# Why this file exists.  cpmtools reads ./diskdefs if there is one and
# /etc/cpmtools/diskdefs (or the homebrew equivalent) otherwise - one or the
# other, never both - and the per-slice combo definitions are not in every
# distribution's copy.  Debian and Ubuntu's cpmtools 2.23 ships wbw_hd1k and
# NOT wbw_hd1k_0, which is the definition hd1k_combo.img needs, so on those
# machines both scripts here reported "r8.com is not on the image at all" for a
# file that is on it.  That is the same wrong-diskdef failure mode the two
# scripts warn about: cpmtools does not fail on a bad format name in a way the
# scripts could tell apart from a missing file.
#
# Because ./diskdefs REPLACES the system file rather than adding to it, this
# has to carry every definition the scripts use, not just the missing ones.
# Both scripts run cpmls/cpmcp/cpmrm with this directory as the working
# directory so that they pick it up wherever they are invoked from.
#
# wbw_hd1k is copied from the stock cpmtools file.  The slice definitions are
# the same geometry with an offset: a combo image is a 1 MB MBR prefix followed
# by whole 8 MB slices (docs/DISK_FORMATS.md).

# RomWBW 8 MB hard disk slice, 1024 directory entries.  A plain single-slice
# image - hd1k_infocom.img - is exactly this and nothing else.
diskdef wbw_hd1k
  seclen 512
  tracks 1024
  sectrk 16
  blocksize 4096
  maxdir 1024
  skew 0
  boottrk 2
  os 2.2
end

# Combo images: the same slice, past the 1 MB MBR prefix and then N whole
# slices.  offset is in bytes: 1048576 + 8388608 * slice.
diskdef wbw_hd1k_0
  seclen 512
  tracks 1024
  sectrk 16
  blocksize 4096
  maxdir 1024
  skew 0
  boottrk 2
  offset 1048576
  os 2.2
end

# Slices 1 and up.  The geometry is the same slice at offset
# 1048576 + 8388608 * n, and that arithmetic is right - an earlier note here
# said it might not be, on the strength of "cannot read superblock (Bad
# parameter)" from every one of them.  What that message actually means is
# measured below.
#
# What the slices hold, checked rather than assumed.  The 32-byte directory
# entry at 1048576 + 8388608*n + 16384 (offset + boottrk 2 * 8192) is an
# ordinary CP/M entry for every n from 0 to 5, and hd1k_combo.img is
# 51380224 bytes = 1 MB + six 8 MB slices exactly.  Cutting each slice out
# with dd and listing it as a plain wbw_hd1k image gives, in order,
# 167/200/301/187/245/66 files and 6144/6052/5112/5720/5592/7036 K free -
# used plus free is 8144K in all six, which is the whole slice less its two
# boot tracks.  Reading the same six through the definitions below gives
# byte-identical files.
#
# So why does it fail here?  Not the definition - the cpmtools BUILD.
# cpmtools 2.23 cannot be configured without libdsk (./configure aborts with
# "No libdsk.h"), and its libdsk backend, device_libdsk.c, flattens
# (track, sector, offset) into ONE linear sector number and hands it to
# dsk_lread with a geometry it derives from `tracks` alone: 1024 tracks
# becomes 512 cylinders x 2 heads x 16 sectors = 16384 sectors = 8 MB.  A
# linear sector at or past that is DSK_ERR_BADPTR, which cpmls prints as
# "Bad parameter".  Measured on this machine, tracks 1024: offset 7340032
# reads the directory, offset 8388608 does not.  A device_posix build of the
# same 2.23 sources reads all six slices correctly - that is what the counts
# above were taken with.
#
# The ceiling is 8 MB FROM THE START OF THE IMAGE, not from the offset, so it
# is not only slices 1 and up that are affected.  wbw_hd1k_0 sits 1 MB in, so
# on a libdsk build the last 1 MB of slice 0 is out of reach.  Measured on a
# COPY of hd1k_combo.img: writing a 5.5 MB file into slice 0 through
# wbw_hd1k_0 fails with "can not write (null): Bad parameter" after 5242880
# bytes and leaves a directory entry for a file that is not all there, and
# reading it back fails at the same byte.  Slice 0 holds 2000K today, so
# nothing has reached it; disks/verify_disk_utils.sh would go loudly red
# rather than quietly wrong if r8.com or w8.com ever landed up there.
#
# What must NOT be done about it: raising `tracks` to cover the offset.  It
# makes the read work and it is the silently-wrong diskdef both scripts exist
# to warn about - tracks 6144 with the slice-1 offset lists slice 1's files
# correctly and then reports 47012K free on an 8 MB slice, so the next write
# would run off the end of the slice and into slice 2.

diskdef wbw_hd1k_1
  seclen 512
  tracks 1024
  sectrk 16
  blocksize 4096
  maxdir 1024
  skew 0
  boottrk 2
  offset 9437184
  os 2.2
end

